4. Finite-Size Percolation Corrections

4.1 Asymptotic versus Finite Percolation

Standard percolation theory—Molloy–Reed criterion—predicts a sharp giant-component threshold  p_c(\infty) = \langle d \rangle / (\langle d^2 \rangle - \langle d \rangle) . In a colony of 50–1000 individuals, the transition is smeared, and using the asymptotic threshold grossly overestimates resilience. We require correction terms that become large for small  N .

Proposal 4.1 (Finite-Size Scaling Ansatz).
The effective critical retention probability for a network of size  N  is

p_c(N) = p_c(\infty) + \Delta p_c(N), \qquad \Delta p_c(N) = A \cdot N^{-1/\nu},

where  \nu  is the correlation-length exponent ( \nu = 3  for mean-field percolation) and  A  depends on the degree distribution. For a power-law degree distribution  P(k) \sim k^{-\gamma} ,  A  can be bounded by Monte Carlo estimation.

4.2 Modularity Penalty

Small groups naturally form clusters. A high global clustering coefficient  \mathcal{C}  retards the emergence of the giant component because edges are trapped inside triangles rather than connecting disparate modules. We introduce the modularity-corrected threshold:

p_c^{\text{eff}}(N, \mathcal{C}) = p_c(\infty) + \Delta p_c(N) + \Omega \cdot \mathcal{C},

with  \Omega > 0  fitted from simulation. This penalty reveals the mathematically precise trade-off between social cohesion (which may benefit psychological well-being) and structural resilience (which requires bridging ties).

4.3 Extreme-Value Fragmentation Time

Rather than a deterministic threshold, we model the time to fragmentation  T_{\text{frag}}  as a random variable. For a finite system, the first passage to the absorbing state follows an extreme-value distribution.

Lemma 4.2 (Gumbel Limit for Fragmentation Time).
Under independent agent turnovers with rate  \mu , and for a network operating near the percolation transition,  T_{\text{frag}}  converges in distribution to a Gumbel variable:

\lim_{N \to \infty} \mathbb{P}\!\left( \frac{T_{\text{frag}} - b_N}{a_N} \le x \right) = \exp(-e^{-x}),

with scale  a_N \propto 1/\sqrt{N}  and location  b_N \propto \ln N . In practice, the CIA targets a reliability constraint  \mathbb{P}(T_{\text{frag}} < T_{\text{mission}}) \le 10^{-4} , which forces the operating point  p  substantially above  p_c(N) .

4.4 Validation Architecture

To implement these corrections, we propose a Monte Carlo configuration-model suite that sweeps  N = 50, 100, 250, 500, 1000  under realistic degree distributions and layered topologies. The output is a Finite-Size Correction Table mapping population size, clustering, and layer coupling to the required safety margin. This table becomes the working reference for the CIA’s percolation clearances.

