6. Structural Divergence and the Ghost Network

Colonies begin with a planned topology  \mathcal{N}_{\text{perf}}  that meets all spectral invariants. Over time, emergent social ties, AI reconfiguration, and unplanned isolations produce an observed topology  \mathcal{N}_{\text{obs}} . The difference between them is a structural divergence that erodes the guarantees of the original design.

Definition 6.1 (Structural Divergence Operator).
Let  W_{\text{perf}}  and  W_{\text{obs}}  be the weighted adjacency matrices of the planned and observed communication-delegation composite graphs, normalised to unit Frobenius norm. The structural divergence is

\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{N}_{\text{perf}}, \mathcal{N}_{\text{obs}}) = \| W_{\text{perf}} - W_{\text{obs}} \|_F.

Lemma 6.2 (Decoherence Threshold).
There exists a threshold  \tau > 0  such that if  \mathcal{D} > \tau , then either  \lambda_2(\mathcal{N}_{\text{obs}}) < \epsilon_{\text{crit}}  or  \rho(A_{\text{obs}}) > \rho_{\text{threshold}} . In other words, structural divergence beyond  \tau  guarantees that at least one spectral invariant leaves the safe envelope.

Proof sketch. The Laplacian and spectral radius are Lipschitz continuous functions of the adjacency matrix under the Frobenius norm. By setting  \tau  small enough, a perturbation bound ensures the eigenvalues remain within the safe intervals.  \blacksquare 

This lemma formalises the Ghost Network concept: the colony is safe only so long as the living network remains within a Frobenius-ball of the reference topology. The CIA’s veto power derives from a direct computation of  \mathcal{D}  and a check against  \tau .

