Appendix A. Multiplex Supra‑Adjacency and Laplacian

A.1 Supra‑adjacency matrix

Let \mathcal{M} = (V, \mathcal{E}_1, \mathcal{E}_2, \mathcal{E}_3) with |V|=N. For each layer \ell\in\{1,2,3\} we have an adjacency matrix W^{(\ell)}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}. The supra‑adjacency matrix \mathbf{S}\in\mathbb{R}^{3N\times 3N} is

\mathbf{S} =
\begin{pmatrix}
W^{(1)} & I & I\\
I & W^{(2)} & 0\\
I & 0 & W^{(3)}
\end{pmatrix},

where I is the N\times N identity matrix (unit inter‑layer coupling). The supra‑Laplacian is \mathcal{L}^{\text{supra}} = \mathcal{D} - \mathbf{S}, with \mathcal{D} the diagonal matrix of row sums of \mathbf{S}.

A.2 Cascade coupling and free parameter

Lemma A.1 (Cascade threshold).
Let S\subset V be a set of nodes removed from layer \ell. The increase in the effective distance between nodes in another layer is governed by the inter‑layer coupling weight. A cascade is declared when the supranode connectivity falls below a calibrated free parameter \eta_{\text{cascade}}, chosen via simulation to match mission risk tolerance. The precise value of \eta_{\text{cascade}} is not derived here but is a control input analogous to \Lambda_{\text{safe}}’s constants.

