Appendix B. Knowledge Hilbert Space and Gramian Redundancy

B.1 Proper construction of knowledge vectors in \mathcal{H}

Let \mathcal{K} be the set of knowledge domains and choose a positive‑definite kernel \kappa:\mathcal{K}\times\mathcal{K}\to\mathbb{R} (e.g. \kappa(k,k')=\exp(-\gamma\|k-k'\|^2) after embedding domains in a metric space). The RKHS \mathcal{H} is the completion of the span of \{\kappa(\cdot,k):k\in\mathcal{K}\}.

For an agent v, instead of assuming that the raw mastery function \Phi(v,\cdot) belongs to \mathcal{H} (a smoothness condition that may fail for discontinuous “all‑or‑nothing” expertise), we construct the agent’s knowledge vector \mathbf{k}_v\in\mathcal{H} as the kernel ridge regression estimator from a set of measured mastery observations D_v=\{(k_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^{m_v}, where y_i = \Phi(v,k_i):

\mathbf{k}_v = \underset{f\in\mathcal{H}}{\arg\min}\;
\frac{1}{m_v}\sum_{i=1}^{m_v} \bigl(f(k_i)-y_i\bigr)^2 + \lambda\|f\|_{\mathcal{H}}^2.

By the representer theorem, \mathbf{k}_v is a finite linear combination \sum_{i=1}^{m_v}\alpha_i \kappa(\cdot,k_i) and therefore always lies in \mathcal{H}. If the true mastery function \Phi(v,\cdot) does belong to \mathcal{H}, the ridge estimate converges to it as m_v\to\infty. In operational terms, the CIA periodically samples an agent’s competency on a reference subset of domains and updates \mathbf{k}_v via the above minimisation.

B.2 Effective redundancy and spanning

Definition B.1 (Gramian and projection).
For a population V with vectors \{\mathbf{k}_v\}, the Gram matrix is G_{ij} = \langle \mathbf{k}_{v_i}, \mathbf{k}_{v_j}\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}. The effective redundancy for a functional requirement \mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{H} is

R_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{f}) = \mathbf{f}^\top \mathbf{K} (\mathbf{K}^\top \mathbf{K})^{-1} \mathbf{K}^\top \mathbf{f},

where \mathbf{K} is the matrix whose columns are the coefficient vectors of \mathbf{k}_v relative to an orthonormal basis.

Theorem B.2 (Minimum agents for \epsilon-coverage).
Given a finite‑dimensional subspace \mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{H} of dimension d, the minimum number of agents required so that every \mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{F} satisfies R_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{f})\ge 1-\epsilon is the solution to

\min_{V'\subseteq V} |V'| \quad\text{s.t.}\quad \sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{K}_{V'}^\top) \ge \sqrt{1-\epsilon},

where \mathbf{K}_{V'} is the restriction of \mathbf{K} to agents in V' and \sigma_{\min} is the smallest singular value. The combinatorial optimisation is NP‑hard in general; in our Monte‑Carlo validation (Appendix I) we use a greedy matching‑pursuit heuristic that yields empirically near‑optimal redundancy, though we do not claim a worst‑case logarithmic approximation. For exact submodular guarantees we refer to the column‑subset‑selection literature (e.g. Çivril & Magdon‑Ismail 2009); a full worst‑case analysis is deferred to future work.

