Appendix C. Proof of Lemma 3.2 (Spectral Governance Lemma)

C.1 Isoperimetric inequality for weighted graphs

Let W be the weighted adjacency matrix of the effective communication graph, D the diagonal degree matrix with d_v = \sum_u w_{vu}, and \mathcal{L}_{\text{norm}} = I - D^{-1/2} W D^{-1/2} the normalised Laplacian with spectrum 0=\lambda_1\le \lambda_2\le\dots\le\lambda_N\le 2. For a subset S\subset V, define

· \mathrm{vol}(S) = \sum_{v\in S} d_v,
· \mathrm{cut}(S,S^c) = \sum_{u\in S, v\notin S} w_{uv}.

The conductance of S is \phi(S) = \frac{\mathrm{cut}(S,S^c)}{\min(\mathrm{vol}(S),\mathrm{vol}(S^c))}, and the Cheeger constant is \phi = \min_S \phi(S).

Lemma C.1 (Cheeger inequality for weighted graphs).
For any weighted undirected graph,

\phi \;\ge\; \frac{\lambda_2}{2}.

Proof. Standard; see Chung (1997), Theorem 2.3. \square

Consequently, for every S\subset V,

\mathrm{cut}(S,S^c) \;\ge\; \frac{\lambda_2}{2}\, \min\bigl(\mathrm{vol}(S),\,\mathrm{vol}(S^c)\bigr). \tag{C.1}

Now let d_{\min} = \min_v d_v and d_{\max} = \max_v d_v. Then \min(\mathrm{vol}(S),\mathrm{vol}(S^c)) \ge d_{\min}\,\min(|S|, N-|S|). Hence for any S with |S| = i \le N/2,

\mathrm{cut}(S,S^c) \;\ge\; \frac{\lambda_2}{2}\, d_{\min}\, i. \tag{C.2}

C.2 Contact‑process model

A critical knowledge item k is present in a subset of agents. Each “infected” node (agent possessing k) teaches each neighbour at rate \beta w_{uv} (so the total teaching rate from set S to S^c is \beta\,\mathrm{cut}(S,S^c)). Simultaneously, each infected node independently loses the knowledge (fails) at rate \mu. The number of infected nodes I(t) evolves as a continuous‑time Markov chain on \{0,\dots,N\} with transitions

i \to i+1 \;\text{at rate}\; \beta\,\mathrm{cut}(S,S^c), \qquad
i \to i-1 \;\text{at rate}\; \mu i,

where S is any set of i infected nodes minimising the cut (the actual rate may be larger; we use the minimum to obtain a conservative bound).

C.3 Coupling to a linear birth‑death process

Lemma C.2 (Stochastic domination).
Assume \beta\frac{\lambda_2}{2} d_{\min} > \mu. Define a linear birth‑death process Y_t on \{0,1,\dots, \lfloor N/2\rfloor\} with birth rate b i and death rate d i, where

b = \beta\frac{\lambda_2}{2} d_{\min}, \qquad d = \mu,

and with an absorbing state at 0 and a reflecting boundary at \lfloor N/2\rfloor (once Y_t reaches \lfloor N/2\rfloor it stays there until possibly dropping). There exists a coupling such that I(t) \ge Y(t) for all t\ge 0 almost surely, provided I(0) = Y(0) \le N/2.

Proof sketch. For i \le N/2, the infection rate in the original process is at least \beta\,\min_{|S|=i}\mathrm{cut}(S,S^c) \ge b i by (C.2). The death rate equals d i. A standard thinning argument (e.g. Liggett 1985, Theorem 2.2) constructs a pair of processes on the same probability space such that every birth in the dominating process can be matched to a birth in the original process, while deaths occur simultaneously. For i > N/2, the original process still has non‑negative infection rate, so it remains above Y_t (which is capped at N/2). \square

C.4 Exponential extinction bound

For the linear birth‑death process Y_t with b>d, the probability that it ever hits 0 starting from i_0 is (d/b)^{i_0} (classical gambler’s ruin). Because I(t)\ge Y(t), the event \{I(t)=0\} implies \{Y(t)=0\}, hence

\mathbb{P}\bigl(I(t)=0 \text{ for some } t\le T\bigr) \;\le\; \left(\frac{d}{b}\right)^{i_0}.

Now suppose the domain initially has redundancy R_0 = i_0 \ge \gamma N (a positive fraction of the colony). Then

\mathbb{P}(\text{extinction}) \;\le\; \left(\frac{2\mu}{\beta\,\lambda_2\, d_{\min}}\right)^{\gamma N}
\;=\; \exp\bigl(-\alpha N\bigr),

with \alpha = \gamma \ln\bigl(\frac{\beta\,\lambda_2\, d_{\min}}{2\mu}\bigr) > 0 because the fraction exceeds 1. This yields the exponential bound claimed in Lemma 3.2, with the sufficiency condition

\lambda_2 \;>\; \frac{2\mu}{\beta\, d_{\min}}.

A complete rigorous treatment for all time horizons (including the unlikely event of extinction after the process has reached macroscopic size) follows from the two‑phase analysis of contact processes on expanders; we refer the reader to Mountford–Valesin–Yao (2016) for the full technical details. The present coupling suffices to establish the exponential scaling in N that the Spectral Governance Lemma requires. \blacksquare


Appendix D. Proof of Theorem 3.3 (Error Amplification Bound)

D.1 Model

The delegation layer is represented by a directed, weighted graph with adjacency matrix A \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}. An edge u \to v with weight a_{vu} indicates that agent v incorporates the output of agent u with trust proportional to a_{vu}. Let \mathbf{e}^{(0)} \in \mathbb{R}^N be an initial error vector, where e^{(0)}_u is the magnitude of a misinformation signal injected at node u (e.g. an AI hallucination). In the linear approximation, the error propagates by the iteration

\mathbf{e}^{(t+1)} = A \,\mathbf{e}^{(t)}, \qquad t = 0,1,2,\dots

Thus \mathbf{e}^{(t)} = A^t \mathbf{e}^{(0)}.

D.2 Spectral radius bound

Let \| \cdot \|_2 denote the Euclidean norm on \mathbb{R}^N and the induced matrix norm. For any square matrix A, Gelfand’s formula gives

\rho(A) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \|A^t\|^{1/t},

where \rho(A) = \max_i |\lambda_i(A)| is the spectral radius. Hence for any \varepsilon > 0 there exists a constant C_\varepsilon such that for all t,

\|A^t\|_2 \le C_\varepsilon \, (\rho(A) + \varepsilon)^t.

Consequently,

\|\mathbf{e}^{(t)}\|_2 \le C_\varepsilon \, (\rho(A) + \varepsilon)^t \, \|\mathbf{e}^{(0)}\|_2. \tag{D.1}

D.3 Decay and amplification regimes

· If \rho(A) < 1, choose \varepsilon small enough so that \rho(A) + \varepsilon < 1. Then \|\mathbf{e}^{(t)}\|_2 \to 0 exponentially fast. The network naturally dampens errors.
· If \rho(A) = 1, the bound does not guarantee decay; the error may persist or grow slowly depending on the Jordan structure.
· If \rho(A) > 1, then for large t, \|\mathbf{e}^{(t)}\|_2 is bounded below by a term proportional to \rho(A)^t (provided the initial error has a component in the eigenspace of the dominant eigenvalue), so errors are exponentially amplified.

D.4 Operational criterion

To prevent unbounded error propagation in the colony, the Collective Intelligence Architect must enforce

\rho(A) \le \rho_{\text{threshold}} < 1,

where \rho_{\text{threshold}} is a safety margin (e.g. 0.95) chosen to allow comfortable exponential decay even under transient overloads. The spectral radius is computed from the observed delegation adjacency matrix; if it exceeds the threshold, countermeasures such as edge pruning or insertion of human-in-the-loop gate nodes (which reduce effective edge weights) are triggered. \blacksquare

