Appendix E. Finite‑Size Percolation Corrections

E.1 Motivation

Classical percolation theory on infinite graphs gives a sharp phase transition at p = p_c(\infty). For a finite network of size N, the transition is smeared over a window of width O(N^{-1/\nu}), and the effective threshold for the emergence of a giant component of size \Theta(N) is shifted to higher p. Using the asymptotic threshold p_c(\infty) without correction dangerously overestimates the connectivity of a small colony.

E.2 Finite‑size scaling ansatz

Let p_c(N) denote the smallest retention probability p such that the expected size of the largest component exceeds 0.5 N. In the mean‑field universality class (or any standard percolation class),

p_c(N) = p_c(\infty) + A \, N^{-1/\nu},

where \nu is the critical exponent of the correlation length (\nu = 3 for mean‑field percolation; \nu \approx 2.4 for three‑dimensional lattices; scale‑free graphs may require separate treatment). The constant A depends on the degree distribution and clustering; we estimate it by Monte‑Carlo simulation (see Appendix I).

E.3 Modularity penalty

Small habitats exhibit high clustering, measured by the global clustering coefficient \mathcal{C}. Triadic closure impedes the spread of connectivity across modules, delaying the giant component. A first‑order correction is

p_c^{\text{eff}}(N, \mathcal{C}) = p_c(\infty) + \Delta p_c(N) + \Omega \, \mathcal{C},

where \Omega is a sensitivity coefficient fitted from simulations. This quantifies the structural trade‑off: social cohesion (high \mathcal{C}) must be balanced against systemic connectivity; the CIA monitors \mathcal{C} alongside other invariants.

E.4 Fragmentation risk and the operational rule

The probability that the colony’s knowledge‑transfer network fragments during a mission of duration T is bounded rigorously by Lemma F.1 (exponential bound). The conjectural Gumbel distribution (§4.3) provides an alternative tool for extreme‑value sensitivity analysis but is not used for formal clearance decisions. The CIA’s percolation clearance certificate is based on:

1. Simulating the finite‑size corrected threshold p_c^{\text{eff}}(N,\mathcal{C}) for the current topology.
2. Ensuring that the current retention probability p (accounting for scheduled rotations and predicted attrition) exceeds the corrected threshold by a margin \Delta_{\text{FS}} chosen so that the exponential bound of Lemma F.1 yields \mathbb{P}(\text{fragmentation}) < 10^{-4}.

E.5 Numerical example

For a colony with N = 150, degree exponent \gamma = 2.5 (scale‑free), clustering \mathcal{C} = 0.3, and asymptotic p_c(\infty) = 0.12, Monte‑Carlo simulations yield A \approx 0.45 and \Omega \approx 0.25, giving

p_c^{\text{eff}} = 0.12 + 0.45\cdot 150^{-1/3} + 0.25\cdot 0.3 \approx 0.12 + 0.10 + 0.075 = 0.295.

Thus a retention rate of at least 0.30 (plus safety margin) is required to maintain connectivity at the required reliability, significantly higher than the asymptotic 0.12. The CIA’s pre‑rotation clearance must guarantee that p remains above this value after any planned departures.

\blacksquare


Appendix F. Fragmentation Time Bounds

Lemma F.1 (Rigorous exponential bound).
Let the initial giant component contain at least \gamma N vertices, and let nodes fail independently at rate \mu. For any mission horizon T,

\mathbb{P}(\text{fragmentation before } T) \;\le\; N \exp\!\Bigl(-c\,\gamma N\,(1-e^{-\mu T})\Bigr),

where c>0 depends on the graph’s expansion. Proof. Removing fewer than \gamma N/2 nodes cannot destroy the giant component. The number of failures in [0,T] is Poisson with mean N(1-e^{-\mu T}). A Chernoff bound yields the exponential tail. \square

This bound is the one used operationally; the Gumbel conjecture (§4.3) serves as a sensitivity tool and is flagged as unproven.


Appendix G. Proof of Lemma 6.2 (Structural Divergence and Stability)

G.1 Definitions and assumptions

Let \mathcal{N}_{\text{perf}} denote the designed reference topology and \mathcal{N}_{\text{obs}} the observed effective communication‑delegation composite. We encode each as a weighted undirected adjacency matrix for the communication layer: W_{\text{perf}} and W_{\text{obs}}. The corresponding normalised Laplacians are

L_{\text{perf}} = I - D_{\text{perf}}^{-1/2} W_{\text{perf}} D_{\text{perf}}^{-1/2}, \qquad
L_{\text{obs}} = I - D_{\text{obs}}^{-1/2} W_{\text{obs}} D_{\text{obs}}^{-1/2},

where D_{\text{perf}} and D_{\text{obs}} are diagonal degree matrices. The structural divergence is measured in Frobenius norm:

\mathcal{D} = \|L_{\text{perf}} - L_{\text{obs}}\|_F.

Additionally, for the delegation layer we consider the directed adjacency matrix A_{\text{perf}} and its observation A_{\text{obs}}; spectral safety depends on the spectral radius \rho(A).

The safe operating envelope is defined by constants \epsilon_{\text{crit}} > 0 (minimum algebraic connectivity) and \rho_{\text{threshold}} > 0 (maximum spectral radius). The reference topology satisfies

\lambda_2(L_{\text{perf}}) > \epsilon_{\text{crit}} + \delta_\lambda, \qquad
\rho(A_{\text{perf}}) < \rho_{\text{threshold}} - \delta_\rho,

for some positive margins \delta_\lambda, \delta_\rho. The lemma asserts that by choosing \tau sufficiently small, \mathcal{D} \le \tau guarantees that the observed invariants remain within the safe envelope.

G.2 Perturbation bound for symmetric matrices

The Hoffman–Wielandt inequality (Horn and Johnson, 1991) states that for any two n \times n real symmetric matrices X and Y,

\sum_{i=1}^{n} \bigl(\lambda_i(X) - \lambda_i(Y)\bigr)^2 \le \|X - Y\|_F^2,

where the eigenvalues are sorted in non‑decreasing order. In particular, for each i,

|\lambda_i(X) - \lambda_i(Y)| \le \|X - Y\|_F. \tag{G.1}

Apply this to X = L_{\text{perf}} and Y = L_{\text{obs}}. Since L_{\text{perf}} and L_{\text{obs}} are symmetric and their eigenvalues lie in [0,2], we obtain

|\lambda_2(L_{\text{obs}}) - \lambda_2(L_{\text{perf}})| \le \mathcal{D}. \tag{G.2}

Hence, if \mathcal{D} \le \tau and we set \tau = \delta_\lambda, then

\lambda_2(L_{\text{obs}}) \ge \lambda_2(L_{\text{perf}}) - \tau > (\epsilon_{\text{crit}} + \delta_\lambda) - \delta_\lambda = \epsilon_{\text{crit}}.

Thus \lambda_2(L_{\text{obs}}) > \epsilon_{\text{crit}} as required.

G.3 Perturbation bound for spectral radius of a non‑symmetric matrix

The spectral radius is not Lipschitz under the Frobenius norm in general, but we have the following elementary bound. For any matrices A, B \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n},

|\rho(A) - \rho(B)| \le \|A - B\|_2 \le \|A - B\|_F,

because the spectral norm \| \cdot \|_2 is bounded above by the Frobenius norm. To see the first inequality, note that for any matrix M, \rho(M) \le \|M\|_2. By the reverse triangle inequality for the spectral norm,

\rho(A) - \rho(B) \le \rho(A-B) \le \|A - B\|_2,

and similarly \rho(B) - \rho(A) \le \|A - B\|_2, so the absolute difference is bounded by \|A - B\|_2. Thus

|\rho(A_{\text{obs}}) - \rho(A_{\text{perf}})| \le \|A_{\text{obs}} - A_{\text{perf}}\|_F. \tag{G.3}

Now, define the Frobenius divergence of the delegation layer as \mathcal{D}_A = \|A_{\text{obs}} - A_{\text{perf}}\|_F. If we ensure that \mathcal{D}_A \le \tau_A with \tau_A = \delta_\rho, then

\rho(A_{\text{obs}}) \le \rho(A_{\text{perf}}) + \tau_A < (\rho_{\text{threshold}} - \delta_\rho) + \delta_\rho = \rho_{\text{threshold}}.

G.4 Combined structural divergence

We can define a single structural divergence metric that merges both layers. Let \mathcal{D}_{\text{total}} = \max\bigl(\mathcal{D}, \mathcal{D}_A\bigr). Choose a threshold \tau = \min(\delta_\lambda, \delta_\rho). Then \mathcal{D}_{\text{total}} \le \tau implies both

\lambda_2(L_{\text{obs}}) > \epsilon_{\text{crit}}, \qquad \rho(A_{\text{obs}}) < \rho_{\text{threshold}}.

Conversely, if the observed topology strays such that \mathcal{D}_{\text{total}} > \tau, then at least one of the invariants may exit the safe envelope—indeed, by the Lipschitz properties derived above, it is guaranteed that the invariant leaves the safe zone if the divergence exceeds the corresponding margin. This proves Lemma 6.2. \blacksquare

G.5 Remarks on robustness

The constants \delta_\lambda and \delta_\rho are chosen by the CIA during the initial network certification so that the Frobenius thresholds correspond to detectable, measurable divergences in the digital twin. The CIA recomputes \mathcal{D} and \mathcal{D}_A periodically; if either approaches \tau, topology restoration protocols are triggered before the spectral invariants actually violate their limits, providing a proactive safety buffer.


Appendix H. Proof of Theorem 5.1 and Corollary 5.2 (Information‑Theoretic Fragility)

H.1 Formal statement of Theorem 5.1

Theorem 5.1 (Non‑attenuating distortion).
Consider a processing chain X_0 \rightarrow X_1 \rightarrow X_2 \rightarrow Y, where X_2 = f(X_1) with f a deterministic, non‑injective mapping. Let P_e = \min_g \mathbb{P}\bigl(g(X_2) \neq X_0\bigr) be the minimum probability of error for recovering X_0 from X_2, and let P_e^{(1)} = \min_h \mathbb{P}\bigl(h(X_1) \neq X_0\bigr) be the minimum probability of error from X_1. Then P_e \ge P_e^{(1)}. In particular, compression cannot reduce the Bayes error.

H.2 Preliminaries

All random variables take values in finite sets. The data‑processing inequality states that if A \rightarrow B \rightarrow C is a Markov chain, then I(A; C) \le I(A; B). In our chain, X_0 \rightarrow X_1 \rightarrow X_2 is Markov (since X_2 = f(X_1)), so

I(X_0; X_2) \le I(X_0; X_1). \tag{H.1}

Fano’s inequality (in its simplest form for hypothesis testing with finite alphabet \mathcal{X}_0) gives: for any estimator \hat{X}_0 that is a function of X_2,

\mathbb{P}(\hat{X}_0 \neq X_0) \ge \frac{H(X_0 \mid X_2) - 1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|}.

Equivalently, the minimum probability of error satisfies

P_e \ge \frac{H(X_0) - I(X_0; X_2) - 1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|}. \tag{H.2}

The same inequality holds for X_1:

P_e^{(1)} \ge \frac{H(X_0) - I(X_0; X_1) - 1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|}. \tag{H.3}

H.3 Proof of the inequality

From (H.1), I(X_0; X_2) \le I(X_0; X_1), so

H(X_0) - I(X_0; X_2) - 1 \ge H(X_0) - I(X_0; X_1) - 1.

Dividing by the positive constant \log |\mathcal{X}_0| preserves the direction, hence

\frac{H(X_0) - I(X_0; X_2) - 1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|} \ge \frac{H(X_0) - I(X_0; X_1) - 1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|}.

Applying (H.2) on the left and (H.3) on the right gives P_e \ge P_e^{(1)}.

Moreover, if f is non‑injective, there exist distinct x_1, x_1' \in \mathcal{X}_1 with f(x_1) = f(x_1'). Choose p(x_0, x_1) such that these two x_1 values lead to different optimal decisions. Then the error increases strictly for that distribution; the inequality is generically strict for at least one prior. \blacksquare

H.4 Proof of Corollary 5.2 (Architectural requirement)

To keep the decision‑stage error P_e below a mission‑critical threshold \epsilon, we need

\frac{H(X_0) - I(X_0; X_2) - 1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|} < \epsilon,

which implies a lower bound on I(X_0; X_2). Since I(X_0; X_2) \le I(X_1; X_2) \le H(X_1), this also bounds the compression loss. Practically, the AI compression step must preserve enough mutual information. This is achieved either by (a) exposing the full posterior distribution (so that X_2 is a sufficient statistic), or (b) providing an audit trail that allows reconstruction of X_1 up to a controlled distortion. The CIA’s audit certifies that the chosen interface satisfies I(X_0; X_2) \ge I_{\min} for the relevant sensor/decision pipeline. \square

