Appendix I. Monte Carlo Configuration Model for Finite‑Size Correction Tables

I.1 Purpose

The finite‑size percolation corrections derived in Appendix E involve constants A (finite‑size scaling shift) and \Omega (modularity penalty) that depend on the network’s degree distribution and clustering. Because analytical values are unavailable for realistic colony topologies, we estimate them via a Monte Carlo configuration‑model suite. The resulting Finite‑Size Correction Table is a reference document from which the CIA reads the required safety margin for a given population size and clustering coefficient.

I.2 Network generation

We generate synthetic colony networks with prescribed size N \in \{50, 100, 250, 500, 1000\}, degree distribution (power‑law P(k) \sim k^{-\gamma} with \gamma \in \{2.2, 2.5, 3.0\}, representing different levels of hierarchy), and target global clustering coefficient \mathcal{C} \in \{0.1, 0.3, 0.5\}.

Algorithm I.1 (Multiplex configuration model with clustering).

1. Degree sequence. For each node, draw a target degree d_i from P(k) and assign half‑edges.
2. Edge creation. While half‑edges remain, choose two uniformly at random and connect, avoiding self‑loops and multiple edges.
3. Clustering. If the current global clustering coefficient is below the target, select a pair of neighbours of a randomly chosen node and connect them, creating a triangle. Repeat until \mathcal{C} is within a tolerance 0.02 of the target or a maximum number of attempts is reached.
4. Multiplex layers. For each layer \ell = 1,2,3, generate a separate graph using the same node set but independent random wiring constrained by the layer’s functional purpose (communication is symmetric, delegation is directed and sparser, knowledge access is bipartite). Inter‑layer identity links are trivial (same node index).

I.3 Percolation experiment

For each generated multiplex \mathcal{M}, we simulate node failure (knowledge retention loss) as follows: each node independently fails (loses all knowledge) with probability q = 1-p. We vary q from 0 to 1 in steps of 0.01. After removal, we compute the size of the largest connected component in the aggregated effective communication‑delegation graph (projected to a single weighted undirected graph by symmetrising the delegation layer and taking the maximum weight across layers). The critical retention probability p_c(N) is defined as the smallest p for which the largest component size exceeds 0.5 N.

We repeat this for 1000 independent realisations of the network and failure sets, recording the mean and standard deviation of p_c(N). The shift \Delta p_c(N) = p_c(N) - p_c(\infty) is then fitted to the form A N^{-1/\nu} with \nu=3 (mean‑field) by least‑squares regression over the N values. The modularity penalty coefficient \Omega is estimated by varying \mathcal{C} at fixed N and fitting the additional shift.

